From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: kebab refactor <kebab@local.invalid>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 15:02:40 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] usb: typec: qcom: stop logging the no-op signal-transmit IRQ
 as an error

qcom_pmic_typec_pdphy_isr() handles PMIC_PDPHY_SIG_TX_IRQ by printing
"isr: tx_sig" at error level and nothing else. The interrupt means the
PHY finished transmitting a hard or cable reset -- a sequence TCPM asked
for and whose completion it already tracks through tcpm_pd_transmit_
complete() and its own timers. There is no error condition here and no
recovery to perform.

Partners that need several resets to negotiate therefore fill the log
with error-level lines that say only that the hardware did as it was
told, which hides the messages that do matter. Demote it to dev_dbg and
record why the handler is empty.
---
 drivers/usb/typec/tcpm/qcom/qcom_pmic_typec_pdphy.c | 11 ++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/usb/typec/tcpm/qcom/qcom_pmic_typec_pdphy.c b/drivers/usb/typec/tcpm/qcom/qcom_pmic_typec_pdphy.c
index e6b28648f..526860b90 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/typec/tcpm/qcom/qcom_pmic_typec_pdphy.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/typec/tcpm/qcom/qcom_pmic_typec_pdphy.c
@@ -379,7 +379,16 @@ static irqreturn_t qcom_pmic_typec_pdphy_isr(int irq, void *dev_id)
 
 	switch (irq_data->virq) {
 	case PMIC_PDPHY_SIG_TX_IRQ:
-		dev_err(dev, "isr: tx_sig\n");
+		/*
+		 * Raised when the PHY has finished putting a signal (hard or
+		 * cable reset) on the wire. TCPM drives those resets itself
+		 * and tracks their completion through its own state machine,
+		 * so there is nothing for this handler to do and nothing has
+		 * gone wrong -- the completion of a reset the stack asked for
+		 * is not an error. Logging it at error level buries real
+		 * failures whenever a partner needs a few resets to come up.
+		 */
+		dev_dbg(dev, "isr: tx_sig\n");
 		break;
 	case PMIC_PDPHY_SIG_RX_IRQ:
 		schedule_work(&pmic_typec_pdphy->reset_work);
